{:kind :reply, :text "", :thinking "Let me analyze this complex task. I need to construct a small live conversation network in Lain. Let me break down the requirements:\n\n1. Build history with temporal reducers (behavior:scan, behavior:source, etc.)\n2. Preserve chronological user messages and acknowledged assistant replies\n3. Connect user-ingress to model requests\n4. Define a reusable reply function that delivers to the user, then adds the acknowledged reply to history\n5. Subsequent requests must contain actual prior user messages and delivered assistant replies as role-tagged messages\n6. Provider thinking and generated source are never conversation replies\n7. Each subsequent model response must be executable Lain calling the reply function with a new assistant message\n8. Conversation requests should include an explicit system message containing the reply-function calling convention\n9. Do not include setup code or provider thinking in conversation history\n10. Use the same model throughout\n11. The parent binding `history` retains generated source code automatically; it is NOT conversation history\n12. Use a different name for the conversation accumulator\n13. memory-cell is a live compound interface with open writable slots\n14. Do not send a request until a user event arrives\n15. Unknown inputs suspend a computation; avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request\n16. model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target\n17. Inspect diagnostics if construction fails\n\nKey constraints from the language-introduction-cell:\n- Lain is NOT Clojure\n- Return raw parenthesized forms only; no triple-backtick fences\n- A callable is invoked as `(f x)`; `(-> x f)` writes TO the callable cell and does NOT call f\n- Only binding/argument vectors use literal brackets; construct data vectors with seq:empty and seq:append\n- Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records or other behavior objects\n- A scan takes its source as its first argument\n- behavior:scan, map, and event-filter produce behavior objects, not ordinary records or vectors\n- Use behavior:latest-value to project a selected behavior value before passing to ordinary primitives\n- An optional fallback makes an empty behavior usable without inventing an occurrence\n- seq:project projects one key, so `(seq:project :role xs)` returns role strings, not message records\n- Model messages must be records with string :role and :content\n- System instructions must be first\n- The user ingress is a message RECORD, not a behavior or callable\n- You must capture its occurrences with event:point if you need a temporal source\n- `(define name expression)` binds a cell\n- `(define pending)` declares an unknown cell\n- `(network [x y] expression)` defines a callable\n- `(let [x expression y expression] body)` binds locals\n- `(-> value target-cell)` connects cells\n- `(if condition yes no)` selects a branch\n- Qualified names avoid editor collisions\n- Use data:record for immutable plain records, NOT struct\n- `(data:record :key value)` constructs a record; `(data:get :key record)` reads it\n- seq:empty [] returns an empty vector; seq:append [vector element] appends one element\n- seq:concat [vector ...] concatenates vectors into one flat vector\n- seq:last [vector] reads the last element\n- seq:project [key vector] projects a key over a vector\n- data:equal? [a b] compares values\n- event:point [id numeric-time value] captures a retained temporal occurrence\n- Each identity is immutable: later changes at its input do not withdraw earlier occurrences\n- behavior:source [] creates a reducer cell; connect supported event:point results to it with ->\n- behavior:scan [source reducer initial] emits a state at EVERY ordered occurrence\n- behavior:map [behavior callable] maps values\n- behavior:event-filter [behavior predicate] keeps matching historical occurrences\n- behavior:latest-value [behavior optional-fallback] projects its most recent value\n- model:request [record] submits { :id string :messages vector-of-role-content-records }\n- user-ingress publishes a record with :id, :time (odd integer), :role user, :content\n- Assistant time should be user time + 1 and assistant id must differ from user id\n- message-to-user [record] acknowledges a record containing :id :time :role assistant :content\n- source:admit [complete-reply lexical-target support-cell-reference] admits complete forms sequentially\n- environment:overview [environment] lists visible binding titles\n- cell:reference [cell] yields an inspectable reference\n- inspect:cell [reference] exposes :edge, :value, :inputs, :outputs\n- cell:value [cell] connects that edge's strongest value directly to an ordinary cell\n- data:snapshot [compound] resolves a finite compound into a plain record\n\nNow, the critical challenge: I need to build a conversation loop where:\n1. user-ingress publishes a record (not a behavior)\n2. I need to capture user-ingress occurrences with event:point to feed a temporal source\n3. But user-ingress is a RECORD, not a behavior - so I need to figure out how to capture it\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"The user ingress is a message RECORD, not a behavior or callable. You must capture its occurrences with event:point if you need a temporal source.\"\n\nSo user-ingress is a cell that holds a record. To make it temporal, I need to use event:point on it. But event:point takes [id numeric-time value]. The value would be the record from user-ingress.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the construction-review-cell: \"A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before feeding a temporal source.\"\n\nAnd: \"Event identity and time must vary with actual events; constants user/1 and assistant/2 cannot identify three turns.\"\n\nSo I need to use the :time from the record as the event time, and the :id as the event identity.\n\nLet me think about the architecture:\n\n1. **User ingress capture**: user-ingress is a cell holding a record. I need to capture its occurrences. Since it's a record (not a behavior), I need to use event:point with the record's :id and :time.\n\n2. **Conversation history**: A behavior:scan that accumulates messages (both user and assistant) as a vector of role-content records.\n\n3. **Model request**: When a user message arrives, construct a request with system message + history, call model:request.\n\n4. **Reply function**: A network that takes an assistant message record, calls message-to-user to deliver it, then adds the acknowledged reply to history.\n\n5. **The loop**: user event → build request → model responds → reply function delivers and updates history → next user event uses updated history.\n\nThe tricky part is avoiding cycles. The construction-review-cell warns: \"avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request.\"\n\nLet me think about how to structure this without cycles:\n\n- user-ingress is a cell that gets written to externally\n- I need to detect when user-ingress changes and trigger a model request\n- The model request needs the current history\n- The reply function needs to update history\n\nThe challenge: how do I trigger a model:request when user-ingress changes? model:request is a function that must be invoked. I can't just connect user-ingress to it.\n\nOne approach: Use a behavior:source for user events, scan to build history, and then... but model:request needs to be called, not connected.\n\nWait, let me re-read the constraints more carefully. \"model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target.\"\n\nSo I need to actually call model:request. But in a propagator network, how do I trigger a function call based on incoming data?\n\nLooking at the examples, model:request is called like: `(model:request (data:record :id \"...\" :messages ...))`\n\nIn a propagator network, if I write to a cell that's used as an argument to model:request, the function should re-evaluate. But the constraint says it must be \"invoked, not used as a connection target.\"\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. In Lain's propagator model:\n- `(define result (model:request input-cell))` - this creates a network where result depends on input-cell. When input-cell gets a value, model:request is called.\n\nActually wait - \"model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target.\" This means I can't do `(-> something model:request)`. But I CAN do `(define result (model:request something))` which invokes it.\n\nSo the pattern would be:\n```\n(define request-payload (build-request user-message history))\n(define model-response (model:request request-payload))\n```\n\nWhen user-message changes, request-payload changes, and model:request is re-invoked.\n\nBut there's a cycle problem: history depends on model-response (through the reply function), and model-response depends on history (through the request). This is the cycle the construction-review-cell warns about.\n\nTo break the cycle: The history should be a behavior:scan that accumulates. The model request should use the LATEST value of history (via behavior:latest-value), not the current scan state directly.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully with the temporal model:\n\n1. user-ingress publishes a record → I capture it as an event:point → feeds a behavior:source\n2. A behavior:scan accumulates all messages (user + assistant) into a vector\n3. When a new user event arrives, I need to call model:request with the current history\n4. The model responds → reply function delivers → acknowledged reply is added to history\n\nThe cycle: history → request → model → reply → history\n\nTo break it: The history scan should only accumulate. The request should read the latest history value. The reply function adds to history. But the reply function's output feeds back into the history scan...\n\nActually, in a temporal model with behavior:scan, this might work:\n- The scan accumulates events over time\n- Each event is either a user message or an assistant reply\n- The scan's current value is the accumulated history\n- When a new user event arrives, we read the scan's latest value to build the request\n- The model responds, the reply function delivers, and the acknowledged reply becomes a new event in the scan\n\nBut the problem is: how does the acknowledged reply become an event in the scan? It needs to go through event:point.\n\nLet me think about the flow:\n1. user-ingress gets a record → event:point captures it → feeds user-source (behavior:source)\n2. user-source feeds a scan that builds the request messages\n3. model:request is called with those messages\n4. The model generates source that calls the reply function\n5. The reply function calls message-to-user → gets acknowledged record\n6. The acknowledged record needs to be captured as an event:point → feeds assistant-source\n7. Both user-source and assistant-source feed into a combined history scan\n\nWait, but step 4-5 involves the model generating source that gets admitted. The source:admit mechanism admits generated code. So the model's response is Lain source that calls the reply function.\n\nLet me reconsider the architecture:\n\nThe reply function is defined in the environment. The model generates source like:\n`(reply-function (data:record :id \"assistant-1\" :time 2 :role \"assistant\" :content \"Hello!\"))`\n\nWhen this source is admitted, it executes, calling message-to-user and adding to history.\n\nSo the flow is:\n1. user-ingress → event:point → user-source → triggers model:request\n2. model:request → model generates source → source:admit → executes reply function\n3. reply function → message-to-user → acknowledged record → event:point → assistant-source\n4. user-source + assistant-source → history scan → feeds next model:request\n\nThe cycle is: history → request → model → source → reply → history\n\nTo avoid the cycle: The history scan's value at time T is used for the request at time T. The reply at time T adds to history for time T+1. Since behavior:scan processes events in order, and the reply event happens AFTER the request event, there's no actual cycle in the temporal ordering.\n\nBut in a propagator network, the dependency graph might still have a cycle. The key insight from the construction-review-cell: \"avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request.\"\n\nThe solution: Use behavior:latest-value with a fallback for the initial state. Before any events, history is empty. The first request uses empty history. After the first reply, history has one entry. The second request uses that history.\n\nLet me now think about the actual Lain code:\n\n```lain\n;; Define the reply function\n(define reply (network [assistant-record]\n  (let [delivered (message-to-user assistant-record)]\n    ;; Add acknowledged reply to history via event:point\n    (-> (event:point (data:get :id delivered) (data:get :time delivered) delivered) assistant-source)\n    delivered)))\n\n;; User source - captures user-ingress occurrences\n(define user-source (behavior:source))\n\n;; Assistant source - captures acknowledged replies\n(define assistant-source (behavior:source))\n\n;; Combined history - scan that accumulates all messages\n(define history-source (behavior:source))\n;; Need to merge user and assistant events into one source...\n```\n\nHmm, but how do I merge two behavior:sources? I can't just connect both to the same source because \"Each call to behavior:source creates a DISTINCT source.\"\n\nWait, I can connect events from both to the same source:\n```\n(-> (event:point ...) combined-source)\n```\n\nBut the events come from different places. Let me think...\n\nActually, I think the approach should be:\n- One behavior:source for all conversation events\n- User events go through event:point → connected to this source\n- Assistant events go through event:point → connected to this source\n- A scan over this source builds the history vector\n\nBut the problem is: how do I connect user-ingress to event:point? user-ingress is a cell holding a record. I need to extract :id and :time from it and create an event:point.\n\n```\n(define user-event (event:point (data:get :id user-ingress) (data:get :time user-ingress) user-ingress))\n(-> user-event conversation-source)\n```\n\nWait, but event:point takes [id numeric-time value]. If user-ingress is a cell, then `(data:get :id user-ingress)` would be a cell that gets the :id from whatever user-ingress holds. And event:point would create an event with that id and time.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: \"Each identity is immutable: later changes at its input do not withdraw earlier occurrences.\" So if user-ingress changes, a new event:point with a new id/time would be a new occurrence.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that event:point creates a single event. If user-ingress changes, the event:point's inputs change, but since \"later changes at its input do not withdraw earlier occurrences,\" the old event remains. But a new event with new id/time would also be created.\n\nHmm, but in a propagator network, if user-ingress changes, the event:point would re-evaluate with new inputs, creating a new event. The old event's identity is preserved.\n\nLet me reconsider. The construction-review-cell says: \"A bare acknowledged record must be converted to a temporal occurrence before feeding a temporal source.\"\n\nSo the pattern is:\n```\n(define user-event (event:point (data:get :id user-ingress) (data:get :time user-ingress) user-ingress))\n(-> user-event conversation-source)\n```\n\nThis should work. When user-ingress gets a new record, a new event:point is created with the new id and time, and it's connected to conversation-source.\n\nNow for the history scan:\n```\n(define conversation-history (behavior:scan conversation-source seq:concat (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nWait, the reducer for scan must be a \"pure two-argument Lain callable.\" seq:concat takes [vector ...] which is variadic. I need a two-argument version.\n\nActually, looking at the scan example: `(behavior:scan doc:scan-input + 0)` uses `+` which is a two-argument function. For building a vector, I could use seq:append: `(behavior:scan source seq:append (seq:empty))`.\n\nBut seq:append takes [vector element] - that's two arguments! Perfect.\n\n```\n(define conversation-history (behavior:scan conversation-source seq:append (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nThis would accumulate each event's value (the message record) into a vector.\n\nNow for the model request. I need to:\n1. Read the latest history\n2. Prepend a system message\n3. Call model:request\n\n```\n(define latest-history (behavior:latest-value conversation-history (seq:empty)))\n(define system-msg (data:record :role \"system\" :content \"Call (reply (data:record :id \\\"assistant-N\\\" :time T :role \\\"assistant\\\" :content \\\"your response\\\"))\"))\n(define request-messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) latest-history))\n(define model-response (model:request (data:record :id \"request-1\" :messages request-messages)))\n```\n\nBut wait - this creates a cycle! model-response depends on latest-history, which depends on conversation-history, which depends on conversation-source, which gets events from the reply function, which is called by the model's generated source, which comes from model-response.\n\nThe construction-review-cell explicitly warns about this: \"avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request.\"\n\nThe solution: The first request should not depend on any reply. Use behavior:latest-value with a fallback of (seq:empty). Before any events, the history is empty. The first request uses empty history. After the first reply adds an event, the history updates. The second request uses that history.\n\nBut in a propagator network, the dependency is still there. The key is that behavior:latest-value with a fallback means: \"before the first occurrence it returns the optional fallback.\" So the first time, it returns (seq:empty) without waiting for any event. This breaks the cycle for the first iteration.\n\nFor subsequent iterations, the history has values from previous events, and the current request uses those. The current reply hasn't happened yet, so there's no cycle.\n\nActually, I think the temporal model handles this naturally. behavior:scan processes events in temporal order. The scan's value at time T only includes events up to time T. The request at time T uses the scan value at time T. The reply at time T+1 adds to the scan for time T+1. No cycle.\n\nBut in a propagator network (which is not strictly temporal), how does this work? The behavior:scan is a temporal construct - it processes events in order. The behavior:latest-value projects the most recent value. So:\n\n- At time 1: user event arrives → scan value = [user-msg-1] → request uses [user-msg-1] → model responds → reply delivers → assistant event at time 2\n- At time 2: assistant event arrives → scan value = [user-msg-1, assistant-msg-1] → next user event at time 3 → request uses [user-msg-1, assistant-msg-1] → ...\n\nThe temporal ordering prevents the cycle. The scan doesn't include future events.\n\nOK, but there's another issue. The model:request is called in the propagator network. When does it get called? It gets called when its inputs change. If I write:\n\n```\n(define model-response (model:request request-payload))\n```\n\nThen whenever request-payload changes, model:request is re-invoked. request-payload depends on latest-history, which depends on the scan. When a new event arrives in the scan, latest-history changes, request-payload changes, and model:request is re-invoked.\n\nBut the model's response is source code that gets admitted. The admitted source calls the reply function. The reply function adds an event to the source. This changes the scan. This changes latest-history. This changes request-payload. This re-invokes model:request.\n\nIs this a cycle? In a temporal sense, no - each invocation happens at a different time. But in a propagator dependency graph, it looks like a cycle.\n\nThe key insight: model:request is an effectful operation (it calls an external model). It's not a pure propagator. The propagator system should handle this by treating it as an external effect that doesn't create a synchronous cycle.\n\nActually, re-reading the constraints: \"model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target.\" This suggests it's called as a function, and its result is a cell. The propagator system handles the invocation.\n\nLet me also consider: \"Do not send a request until a user event arrives.\" This means the first model:request should not fire until user-ingress has a value. I can achieve this by making the request depend on user-ingress being known.\n\nHmm, but if I use behavior:latest-value with a fallback, the request would fire immediately with empty history. I need to gate it on user-ingress.\n\nOne approach: Use a behavior:source for user events, and only trigger the request when a user event arrives. But model:request needs to be invoked...\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. In the propagator model:\n- `(define x (model:request payload))` creates a cell x that depends on payload\n- When payload gets a value, model:request is called\n- If payload is unknown, x is unknown (suspended)\n\nSo if I make the request payload depend on user-ingress (which starts unknown), the request won't fire until user-ingress has a value. \n\nBut I also need the history. If history starts as (seq:empty) via the fallback, and user-ingress is unknown, then the payload is unknown (because it depends on user-ingress), and the request is suspended. When user-ingress gets a value, the payload becomes known, and the request fires.\n\nLet me try to structure this:\n\n```lain\n;; Sources for temporal events\n(define conversation-source (behavior:source))\n\n;; Capture user-ingress as a temporal event\n(define user-event (event:point (data:get :id user-ingress) (data:get :time user-ingress) user-ingress))\n(-> user-event conversation-source)\n\n;; History scan - accumulates message records\n(define conversation-history (behavior:scan conversation-source seq:append (seq:empty)))\n\n;; Latest history value (with empty fallback for before first event)\n(define latest-history (behavior:latest-value conversation-history (seq:empty)))\n\n;; System message\n(define system-instruction (data:record :role \"system\" :content \"To reply, call: (reply (data:record :id \\\"assistant-N\\\" :time T :role \\\"assistant\\\" :content \\\"your text\\\"))\"))\n\n;; Build request messages: system + history\n(define request-messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-instruction) latest-history))\n\n;; Request payload - depends on user-ingress being known\n(define request-payload (data:record :id (data:get :id user-ingress) :messages request-messages))\n\n;; Model request - fires when user-ingress is known\n(define model-response (model:request request-payload))\n```\n\nWait, but this has a problem. The request-payload depends on user-ingress AND latest-history. latest-history depends on conversation-history which depends on conversation-source. conversation-source gets events from user-event (which depends on user-ingress) AND from the reply function.\n\nThe reply function is called by the model's generated source. The model's generated source comes from model-response. model-response depends on request-payload. request-payload depends on latest-history. latest-history depends on conversation-history. conversation-history depends on conversation-source. conversation-source gets events from the reply function.\n\nSo: model-response → (generated source) → reply → event → conversation-source → conversation-history → latest-history → request-payload → model-response\n\nThis IS a cycle in the dependency graph!\n\nThe construction-review-cell says: \"avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request.\"\n\nThe key: \"before the first request.\" The cycle is broken because:\n1. First request: latest-history = (seq:empty) (fallback, no events yet). No cycle.\n2. Model responds, reply adds event. History updates.\n3. Second request: latest-history = [msg1, msg2]. The reply that created msg2 happened BEFORE this request. No cycle.\n\nIn a temporal model, this works because events are ordered. In a propagator model, behavior:scan and behavior:latest-value handle the temporal ordering. The scan's value at any point only includes events that have already occurred.\n\nBut I'm worried about the propagator system seeing a cycle. Let me re-read: \"Unknown inputs suspend a computation; avoid cycles requiring a future reply before the first request.\"\n\nI think the key is that behavior:latest-value with a fallback means: \"before the first occurrence it returns the optional fallback, or remains unknown if no fallback was supplied.\" So with the fallback, it's never unknown - it always has a value. This means the dependency on latest-history doesn't block the first request.\n\nBut the cycle still exists in the graph. The propagator system might detect it. However, since behavior:scan is a temporal construct that processes events in order, and behavior:latest-value projects the current state, the cycle is \"broken\" by the temporal semantics.\n\nI think this is the intended design. The construction-review-cell's warning is about a different kind of cycle - one where you literally need the reply before you can make the request. With the temporal model, you don't - you use the history up to the current point.\n\nLet me also address: \"Do not send a request until a user event arrives.\"\n\nIf I make request-payload depend on user-ingress (which starts unknown), the request is suspended until user-ingress has a value. But I also need it to depend on the history. If history has a fallback, it's always known. So the request fires when user-ingress becomes known.\n\nActually wait - if I use `(data:get :id user-ingress)` in the request-payload, and user-ingress is unknown, then request-payload is unknown, and model:request is suspended. When user-ingress gets a value, request-payload becomes known, and model:request fires. Good.\n\nBut for subsequent requests, user-ingress might still hold the same value (or a new one). If it holds the same value, the request-payload doesn't change, and model:request isn't re-invoked. I need a new user event to trigger a new request.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. In a propagator network, if user-ingress doesn't change, nothing re-fires. I need each user message to be a distinct event.\n\nThe event:point mechanism handles this: \"Each identity is immutable: later changes at its input do not withdraw earlier occurrences.\" So if user-ingress changes to a new record with a new :id and :time, a new event:point is created, which is a new occurrence in the source.\n\nBut the request-payload depends on user-ingress directly (for the :id). If user-ingress changes, request-payload changes, and model:request re-fires. But it also depends on latest-history, which changes when the reply event arrives. So the request re-fires when history changes too.\n\nWait, I don't want the request to re-fire when history changes (that would be the cycle). I want it to fire only when a new user message arrives.\n\nLet me restructure: The request should be triggered by user events, not by history changes. But it needs to READ the current history.\n\nOne approach: Use behavior:map on the user-source to build the request. The map function reads the latest history and constructs the request.\n\n```\n(define request-builder (network [user-record]\n  (let [hist (behavior:latest-value conversation-history (seq:empty))]\n    (data:record :id (data:get :id user-record) :messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-instruction) hist)))))\n\n(define request-behavior (behavior:map user-source request-builder))\n(define request-value (behavior:latest-value request-behavior))\n(define model-response (model:request request-value))\n```\n\nBut wait - \"behavior:map [behavior callable] maps values\" and \"A callable inside behavior:map or scan is evaluated as a pure transformation; model:request and message-to-user must run in the live network, never inside a temporal transformation callback.\"\n\nSo I can't call model:request inside behavior:map. But I CAN read behavior:latest-value inside it? Actually, \"reading changing external cells does not read the live network\" in callbacks. So behavior:latest-value inside a callback might not work as expected.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me re-read: \"Callback invocation uses a frozen temporary network, so callbacks must use their explicit arguments and immutable constants; reading changing external cells does not read the live network.\"\n\nSo inside behavior:map's callable, I can't read the live history. I can only use the argument (the user record) and constants.\n\nThis means I can't build the request inside behavior:map. I need to build it in the live network.\n\nAlternative approach: Use the propagator model directly.\n\n```\n;; user-ingress is a cell that gets written to externally\n;; When it changes, we want to trigger a model request\n\n;; The request payload depends on user-ingress and the current history\n;; But we need to avoid the cycle\n\n;; Approach: The request fires when user-ingress changes.\n;; The history is read via behavior:latest-value (which has a fallback, so it's always known).\n;; The cycle is broken because behavior:scan processes events in temporal order.\n\n(define request-payload (data:record :id (data:get :id user-ingress) :messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-instruction) latest-history)))\n(define model-response (model:request request-payload))\n```\n\nThe cycle: model-response → source:admit → reply → event → conversation-source → conversation-history → latest-history → request-payload → model-response\n\nBut behavior:latest-value with a fallback means latest-history is always known. And behavior:scan processes events in order. So the \"cycle\" is actually a temporal sequence, not a synchronous cycle.\n\nI think this is the intended design. The propagator system should handle this because:\n1. behavior:scan is a temporal construct - it doesn't create a synchronous dependency\n2. behavior:latest-value projects the current state - it's a snapshot, not a live dependency\n3. The fallback ensures the first request doesn't block\n\nLet me also think about the reply function:\n\n```\n(define reply (network [assistant-record]\n  (let [acknowledged (message-to-user assistant-record)]\n    (-> (event:point (data:get :id acknowledged) (data:get :time acknowledged) acknowledged) conversation-source)\n    acknowledged)))\n```\n\nWait, but \"message-to-user [record] acknowledges a record containing :id :time :role assistant :content and returns that record with :acknowledged true.\"\n\nAnd \"Its output, not generated source, is the delivered message.\"\n\nSo message-to-user returns the record with :acknowledged true. I need to add this to history.\n\nBut the reply function is called by the model's generated source. The generated source is admitted via source:admit. When the source is admitted, the reply function is called, which calls message-to-user, which returns the acknowledged record. Then I need to add it to the conversation source.\n\nThe issue: `(-> (event:point ...) conversation-source)` inside the reply function. This connects the event to the source. But the reply function is a network - when it's called, the -> connection is made.\n\nActually, in a propagator network, `(-> value target)` is a connection declaration. Inside a network, it creates a connection within that network's scope. When the network is called, the connection is established.\n\nHmm, but I need the event to go to the OUTER conversation-s